// Exercises: `tcp`, `connect`, `send`/`recv` (reused from `chan`), `stop`
// (reused from `sandbox`). A raw TCP client -- connects to *any* service
// speaking a text protocol, not just another Nirdosha process. This is
// the actual mechanism behind "orchestrate an arbitrary containerized
// workload": Nirdosha doesn't need to speak the target's language or run
// its interpreter inside the container -- it just needs a socket.
//
// Needs something listening on 127.0.0.1:8000 first, e.g.:
//   python3 -m http.server 8000
//
// `recv` is one read syscall, not a loop until a full response arrives
// (see `read_tcp`'s doc comment in interpreter.rs) -- for a small enough
// response this is the whole reply in one call; a larger or chunked one
// would need a read loop and string concatenation, neither of which
// exists yet.
fn main() {
    let conn: tcp = connect("127.0.0.1", 8000)
    send(conn, "GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: localhost\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n")
    let response: str = recv(conn)
    stop conn
    print(response)
}
